  "query_preview": "..(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 357 PM EDT WED JUL 29 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Friday morning with an upper-level trough\naxis extending from Atlantic Canada southward along the Atlantic\nSeaboard. Meanwhile, an ~588 dam high will be in place over \nArizona and New Mexico with broad ridging extending to the north \nacross the Canadian Prairies. Less prominent ridging extends east\nand northeast from the high to across the Mid-Mississippi Valley,\nOhio Valley, and beyond into Southwest Quebec. A sharpening and \ndigging upper-level shortwave trough is also sliding across the \nUpper Midwest through the eastern side of the ridge. At the \nsurface, high pressure, centered over West Virginia, dominates the\nthe Central Appalachians and into adjacent regions. Meanwhile, \nlow pressure is consolidating from Kansas to Iowa ahead of the \nshortwave trough (and will become our most significant weather \nmaker of the forecast period).\n\nThe surface high pressure system and associated shortwave ridging\naloft will shift east to along the Atlantic Seaboard by the end \nof the day Friday while the deepening 500 hPa shortwave dives \nsoutheast and the attendant surface low tracks across the mid- \nMississippi Valley, moving directly over the Commonwealth Saturday\nnight. Some of the energy within this trough will be captured by \na broader 500 hPa trough pivoting through Eastern Canada and the \nGreat Lakes. Guidance suggests whatever energy remains pinches off\ninto a closed low that then retrogrades toward the Lower Mississippi\nValley early next week and becomes entrapped under the eastward- \nbuilding 500 hPa ridging. As a result, the surface low will \nocclude, sink southward, and decay on Monday. Meanwhile, a cold \nfront associated with the trough passing through the Northeast \nwill brush to our north and east.\n\nAs the low pressure system approaches, southerly flow will send PWATs\nrising to 1.75+ inches (around 80th percentile relative to climo) on\nSaturday, but those higher values will be short-lived overall as\ndrier air sinks back in from the north behind the retrograding low\nand trough brushing to our north. WPC has a Marginal to Slight \nRisk for Excessive Rainfall over the JKL CWA on Saturday and \nSaturday night to account for the potential of locally heavy \nrainfall, which is the main concern in the long-term period. \nHowever, several days of antecedent drying as well as the overall\nprogressive nature of the system should help mitigate hydro \nconcerns. Fortunately, the instability and shear parameter space \nremains weak and that should preclude any notable severe \nconvective weather threat.\n\nSensibly, look for increasing clouds on Friday and Friday night with\nhigh temperatures in the lower to mid 80s and low temperatures in\nthe lower to middle 60s. Rain chances return on Saturday and peak\nat categorical PoPs Saturday night and early Sunday morning. \nDaily rain chances follow for Monday through Wednesday, but \nconvective coverage should be more sparse and modulated by the \ndiurnal heating cycle. Temperatures will continue to run in the \nlower to middle 80s for highs each afternoon (except upper 70s for\nsome locations on Sunday), while nighttime lows dip into the 65 \nto 70 degree range for most.",
